00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.670 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  hi  i am zj i'm a data scientist based in  melbourne  i want to talk about this package called  disk frame have you ever encountered  this message cannot allocate vectors of  size  whatever so what is the issue here well  the issue is that r tries to load the  whole data  into ram so  it doesn't really fit sometimes if your  data is quite large how do you deal with  that  in this talk i'll talk about this frame  but apache spark  by for example sparkly r is also  possible  so how does disk frame work well disk  frame  is very simple it's just a folder with  many  fst files if you're not familiar with  fst files  see this website each file is called a  chunk  and basically if your data set is too  large  i don't have to load everything to  memory i just break that whole data set  into smaller chunks and load it chunk by  chunk  now once i can load the data chunk by  chunk i can do many things for example  i can operate on the chunks in parallel  so how do you make use of this frame  well the first thing you want to do is  load some data into the disk frame  format  so the disk frame package has this  convenience function called csv to disk  frame  you simply provide a path to the csv  file  and it will convert that to a disk frame  if you have multiple csvs you can just  pass it a  list of csvs or a vector of csv  paths well i'm going to show you a demo  using 1.8 billion rows so i've  downloaded the fannie mae data set so  the first thing you should do when you  started this frame is run this function  called setup disk frame  this will set up parallel processing for  you as i mentioned before i've created a  folder with many  fsd files in this directory so i can  show you the content of this directory  as you can see it's just a bunch of fst  files  so to make use of these fst files i just  call this function called disk frame  and i'm going to click the head and tail  just like a normal data frame i can see  things like the end row so you can see  this 1.8 billion rows in this data set  and the csv unzipped would be about 200  gig in size  so there's some more functions number of  columns and the names of the columns  so you can operate a disk frame almost  like as if you have a data frame one of  the key features of disk frame is that  i've implemented the deployer verbs  so we can make use of the player verbs  directly to manipulate the data  so i start with the disk screen i  created  i run this function called source keep  and this is very important this is  actually what makes this frame  fast so a source key basically tells  the program which columns to load into  memory  because there's 31 columns for this  particular analysis i only need  these four columns so i only need to  load these four columns into  ram so i just filter  like you would normally do with the  player to mutate  and then group by year which is created  here  and do a summarize and finally collect  so let's run that  that's running i will show you  what's happening is with the task  manager  most of the cpus are being utilized and  that's because it's processing all the  chunks in that data set in parallel well  as you can  see i've done a group by and created new  columns  in the meantime in about one minute this  was done on the fenime data set with 1.8  billion rows to learn more about this  frame  simply goes to this cream.com i like to  finish off by  pointing out a couple of things if you  do  have spark it's perfectly fine to use it  but not everyone's lucky enough to have  a spark cluster set up  so this frame you can use r functions  directly even those defined by the user  thank you

